<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laocoön and His Sons</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laocoön Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Laocoön and His Sons is a marble sculpture showing the Trojan priest Laocoön and his sons attacked by sea serpents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> attributed it to the Rhodian sculptors Hagesander, Athenodorus and Polydorus. Rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1506 and acquired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, it is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-museums">Vatican Museums</a> and deeply influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> artists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laocoon-and-his-sons.jpg" alt="Laocoön and His Sons" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilfredor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laoco%C3%B6n_and_his_sons_group.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
